-- title:
--   Stopping rules of the infinite-horizon model
-- statement:
--   A **stopping rule** of the infinite-horizon model is a stopping time $\tau$ with values in $\mathbb N\cup\{\infty\}$ for the observations $((Y_j,U_j))_{j\ge1}$: for each $i$, the event $\{\tau=i\}$ depends only on the first $i$ stage pairs. The value $\infty$ means that nothing is ever accepted, with payoff $X_\infty:=0$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Stages are numbered from $0$; the coins are part of the observation, so randomized rules are included.
-- source:
--   Kim–Perchet, Effective Horizons for Prophet Inequalities with Discounted I.I.D. Rewards, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 2ee8cccf…), Section 7, p.11; A.10, p.50

import Mathlib

namespace EffHorizonProphet.Supplement

/-- A **stopping rule of the infinite-horizon model** (Kim–Perchet, *Effective Horizons for Prophet Inequalities with Discounted I.I.D. Rewards*, STOC 2027
submission (manuscript sha256 2ee8cccf…), Section 7, p.11; A.10, p.50): a
stopping time with values in `ℕ ∪ {∞}`, where `∞` means that no reward is ever accepted.

**Formalization Note** Stages are `0`-based and `τ ω = ⊤` is "never stop" (payoff `X_∞ := 0`).
The event `{τ = i}` must be measurable for the σ-algebra generated by the stage pairs `(Y_j, U_j)`,
`j ≤ i`. This filtration contains the paper's `σ(Y_1, …, Y_i)`, so randomized rules are
included. -/
def IsInfStoppingRule (τ : (ℕ → ℝ × unitInterval) → ℕ∞) : Prop :=
  ∀ i : ℕ,
    @MeasurableSet _ (MeasurableSpace.comap
      (fun ω : ℕ → ℝ × unitInterval => fun j : {j : ℕ // j ≤ i} => ω j)
      MeasurableSpace.pi) {ω | τ ω = i}

end EffHorizonProphet.Supplement


